<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karabel relief</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Karabel monument</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karabel relief is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> rock carving at the Karabel pass near Izmir in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, showing a warrior with bow and spear accompanied by a Luwian hieroglyphic inscription. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> took it for an image of the Egyptian king Sesostris. A reading of the inscription published in 1998 identified the figure as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarkasnawa">Tarkasnawa</a>, king of Mira, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, which helped fix the location of the Arzawa lands in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karabel-relief.jpg" alt="Karabel relief" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karabel1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
